# {{Name}} development guide

## Product and boundaries

{{Name}} is an independently developed PowerShell plugin for **Gerber Core**,
the host application. Integrate only through **Gerber Plugin API v1**, supplied
to `Get-GerberPlugin -Gerber $Gerber`. Never call private Core implementation
functions, import Core source, or depend on another product plugin.

This guide is canonical for every coding agent. `CLAUDE.md` and
`.github/copilot-instructions.md` are bridges; `.github/instructions/` contains
file-scoped rules, `.github/agents/` defines a development role, and
`.github/skills/` contains task workflows. Read the matching scoped guidance
without maintaining another copy of this guide.

## Automated API boundary check

Run `./scripts/Test-PluginBoundary.ps1` before the normal Nova build/test flow.
It imports installed Gerber and invokes its public `Test-GerberPluginBoundary`
command on this project; it needs neither a Core checkout nor an active Gerber
session or plugin installation. Inspect the implemented public operation inventory
with `(Test-GerberPluginBoundary -Path .).PublicApi` and use the contract below
for signatures. If no operation fits, request a public API capability; never
dot-source private Core files, enter Core module scope or import implementation
modules to work around a diagnostic.

The AST check reports deterministic file/line/dependency/reason/resolution
diagnostics for known private calls and explicit implementation imports. It
respects plugin-owned function declarations and ignores ordinary strings/comments.
Computed names, aliases, arbitrary dataflow and external code still need review.
Passing is architecture evidence, not permissions, runtime isolation or a sandbox.
No user approval, discovery, enable/disable or execution policy is changed.

## Authored source versus generated output

| Path | Ownership |
| --- | --- |
| `project.json` | Authored NovaModuleTools project configuration: name, description, version, manifest, coverage and packaging settings. |
| `src/public/Get-GerberPlugin.ps1` | Authored public registration entry point; one exported function per matching file. |
| `src/private/` | Authored command definitions and handler factories; not exported. |
| `tests/public/` | Source unit tests and built-module `.Integration.Tests.ps1` tests. |
| `tests/private/` | Source-mirrored unit tests of definitions and handlers. |
| `tests/PluginTestSupport.ps1` | Shared source-loading/test support. |
| `scripts/Test-PluginBoundary.ps1` | Development boundary gate using installed Gerber's public validator. |
| `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` | Authored user guidance and unreleased changes. |
| `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.github/` | Authored agent guidance, not module runtime dependencies. |
| `dist/{{Name}}/` | Nova-generated `.psm1` root module, `.psd1` manifest and packaged resources. Do not hand-edit. |
| `docs/schema/v0/project.json` | Nova-generated project schema. Do not treat it as authored configuration. |
| `artifacts/` | Generated test results, JaCoCo coverage and package output when configured. |

PowerShell **7.4** is the minimum in `Manifest.PowerShellHostVersion`; do not
introduce syntax or APIs requiring a newer runtime without an explicit support
change. Nova generates exports from `src/public/`; same-file sibling functions
are not language-enforced private. Shared helpers belong in `src/private/`.

## Identity and lifecycle

`project.json` **Manifest.GUID** is the permanent identity: `{{Guid}}`.
Keep it stable across versions. Changing it creates a new approval and secret
identity, not an update. Nova propagates it into the built module manifest.
The public plugin definition's `Guid` must match that manifest GUID.

`Manifest.PrivateData.Gerber` contains `Plugin: true`, `ApiVersion: "1"` and
`MinimumGerberVersion: "0.1.0-preview"`. Do not add a second GUID there.
The module name has the fixed `Gerber.` prefix; the command namespace
`/{{Namespace}}` is a separate registration path, not an approval identity.

Gerber approves GUIDs and persists association/enabled state. Successful
creation approved, associated and enabled this GUID in the creating user's
configuration, but did **not** install or load it. `/plugins list` and
`/plugins info` are read-only; known name/version metadata can appear while the
state is `Missing` and `Loaded` is `No`. Installed manifest metadata takes
precedence. After installation, restart Gerber for discovery.
Another user's configuration requires its own approval. `/plugins disable`
preserves approval and association but prevents next-startup loading;
`/plugins enable` also supports a previously declined plugin. Do not invent
removal/revocation workflows or edit Core's configuration directly.

## Implement and register a command

Use the working `/{{Namespace}} hello` example, not a private Core repository:

1. Inspect `Get-GerberPlugin.ps1`, `Get-PluginCommandDefinitionList.ps1`,
   `Get-PluginHelloHandler.ps1` and `Get-PluginMenuHandler.ps1`.
2. Add a small handler factory under `src/private/`, returning a scriptblock
   with `param($Command)`. Capture `$Gerber` with `.GetNewClosure()` as in
   `Get-PluginHelloHandler`. Return `$false` to stay in Gerber, not `$true`
   (which requests application exit). Do not leak incidental pipeline output.
3. Register a leaf such as `{{Namespace}} status` in
   `Get-PluginCommandDefinitionList`. Definitions have `Name`, `Description`,
   `Handler` (scriptblock) and `Subcommands` (dictionary). Names omit `/`.
   Reuse `Get-PluginCommandDefinition`; preserve the namespace parent and
   avoid collisions with Core or another plugin.
4. Wire the parent menu's ordered action table and dispatch in
   `Get-PluginMenuHandler`; adding a leaf does not automatically update this
   hand-authored menu. Use `UI.ReadSubcommand` so direct slash invocation and
   interactive navigation stay consistent. Escape/back is not success.
5. Add source-mirrored unit tests for the factory, definition and menu routing.
   Stub the supplied API operations as the existing tests do. Test arguments,
   output, cancellation and recoverable failures before implementation.
6. Extend `tests/public/Get-GerberPlugin.Integration.Tests.ps1`: import the
   built plugin, check entry point/manifest identity and execute the handler
   using a supplied public API fixture. No real credentials are needed.
7. Run the Nova validation below, update README examples and the appropriate
   `[Unreleased]` changelog category. Locally publish only with authorization,
   restart Gerber and invoke `/{{Namespace}} status` in a test-owned host.

## Public Plugin API v1 contract

The injected context has `ApiVersion`, `PluginId`, `UI`, `Ado`, `Secrets` and
`Clipboard`. Operations are **scriptblocks**, invoked with `&`, not cmdlets
or object methods. The example needs only:

```powershell
& $Gerber.UI.ShowTextBlock '/{{Namespace}} hello' 'Hello from {{Name}}!'
$null = & $Gerber.UI.WaitForContinue
$action = & $Gerber.UI.ReadSubcommand '{{Namespace}}' $Command ([ordered]@{ hello = 'Say hello' })
```

Available capability families (signatures show positional argument order):

| Capability | Supported operations |
| --- | --- |
| `UI` | `ReadSubcommand(name, command, table)`, `ShowTextBlock(title, text)`, `ShowScreenLines(title, lines)`, `StartScreen(name)`, `WriteScreenLine(text)`, `WaitForContinue()`. |
| `UI` input | `ReadCommand(prompt, suggestionMode, inputMode, promptContext, trimResult)`, `ReadSecretText(prompt)`, `ReadNumberedMenuSelection(prompt, title, text, items)`, `ReadNumberedMenuPrompt(prompt, title, text, items)`, `ReadCheckboxMenuPrompt(request)`, `ReadConfirmationPrompt(prompt, title, text, options)`. |
| `UI` feedback | `WriteProgressBar(current, total, prefix, overwritePreviousRender)`, `WritePromptTrace(eventName, data)`. |
| `Ado` connection/GET | `OpenConnection(commandLabel, profileName = '')`, `NewProjectUri(connection, relativePath, queryMap)`, `InvokeGet(connection, uri, requestLabel)`. |
| `Ado` pull requests | `ResolvePullRequestReference(connection, inputText)`, `GetPullRequest(connection, reference)`, `GetPullRequestChangedFileList(connection, pullRequest)`, `GetPullRequestFileContent(connection, pullRequest, path, allowMissing = false)`, `GetPullRequestBuildValidationStatus(connection, pullRequest)`. |
| `Ado` pipelines | `GetPendingApprovalList(connection, environmentId)`, `GetEnvironmentList(connection)`, `ResolvePipelineDefinitionFromUrl(connection, pipelineUrl)`, `GetPipelineRunList(request)`, `StartPipelineRun(request)`, `WaitPipelineRunCompletion(request)`, `ApprovePipelineApproval(connection, approvalId, comment)`, `StopPipelineRun(connection, buildId)`. |
| `Ado` build metadata | `GetBuildTagList(connection, buildId)`, `AddBuildTagIfMissing(connection, buildId, tag)`, `RemoveBuildTag(connection, buildId, tag)`, `AddBuildRetentionLeaseIfMissing(connection, definitionId, buildId)`, `GetBuildRunUrl(connection, buildId)`. |
| `Ado` work items | `GetWorkItemIdFromUrl(url)`, `GetWorkItemFieldMap(request)`, `UpdateWorkItemFields(request)`. |
| `Secrets` | `EnsureReady(actionLabel, screenTitle)`, `Test(pathSegments)`, `GetText(pathSegments)`, `Set(pathSegments, secret)`, `Remove(pathSegments)`. |
| `Clipboard` | `CopyText(value)`. |

For an optional ADO command:

```powershell
$connection = & $Gerber.Ado.OpenConnection '/{{Namespace}} status'
$uri = & $Gerber.Ado.NewProjectUri $connection '_apis/build/definitions' ([ordered]@{})
$definitions = & $Gerber.Ado.InvokeGet $connection $uri 'Read build definitions'
```

`OpenConnection` without a profile uses the default/readiness flow; an explicit
profile selects only that profile, never silently falls back. The returned
connection exposes non-sensitive metadata such as `ProfileName`, `ServerUri`,
`CollectionUri`, `ProjectName` and `ProjectUri`; it contains no PAT/token.
GET and mutations obtain authentication lazily inside Core. Request-object
operations bind a `Connection`; their additional operation-specific fields
must be verified against the host's public API reference before use. Do not
guess request fields or treat this capability inventory as a request schema.

Secrets use logical string-array paths owned by this manifest GUID:
`& $Gerber.Secrets.GetText @('service', 'key')`. Never access Core config,
Keychain/Credential Manager, PAT storage or credentials directly. Do not log
secret values. Plugins execute in the host process; this API is not a sandbox.
Mock network, secrets and destructive operations in tests. New external-system
features need their own reviewed public contract; do not invent capabilities.
Use `& $Gerber.Clipboard.CopyText $value` for clipboard output rather than
platform-specific clipboard commands.

## Verified NovaModuleTools workflow

NovaModuleTools 3.4.1 provides the commands below. Install it explicitly only if
missing (`Install-Module -Name NovaModuleTools`), then run from the project root:

```powershell
Import-Module NovaModuleTools
Get-NovaProjectInfo
Invoke-NovaBuild
Invoke-NovaTest
Test-NovaBuild
```

`Get-NovaProjectInfo` loads/validates project configuration; build additionally
validates source/export layout. Unit tests use Pester and the project-configured
**90%** source coverage gate. Integration tests validate the built module.
Do not bypass Nova by running Pester directly or disabling coverage to get green.
Pester and PSScriptAnalyzer are development tools, not plugin runtime dependencies.

Before handoff, inspect the installed PSScriptAnalyzer and run it on authored
PowerShell sources/tests (no analyzer wrapper or CI workflow is generated):

```powershell
Invoke-ScriptAnalyzer -Path ./src -Recurse
Invoke-ScriptAnalyzer -Path ./tests -Recurse
```

Resolve findings, don't suppress them. Keep units short/cohesive, interfaces
small, branches shallow and business/test logic nonduplicated (SIG). Where
CodeScene is available, record pre/post `code_health_review` and
`code_health_score`, target 10.0 for all touched supported source/tests/helpers,
and run `pre_commit_code_health_safeguard` before a commit. Missing tooling is
unverified, not a passing check. No CI/CodeScene integration is preconfigured.

With explicit permission to install, isolate automated publication:

```powershell
Publish-NovaModule -Local -ModuleDirectoryPath '/path/to/test-owned/modules'
```

For the developer's normal local module directory:

```powershell
Publish-NovaModule -Local
```

Local publication builds/tests and replaces an existing same-name module at
its destination; use a separate process from a Pester host. Add an isolated
module root to the fresh host's `PSModulePath`, keep its Gerber config separate,
restart Gerber and run `/{{Namespace}} hello`. Generation alone is not install.

For **separately authorized** version/package/release work, verify installed
help first (`Get-Command <command> -Syntax`, `Get-Help <command> -Full`):

- `Update-NovaModuleVersion -Path . -WhatIf` previews the changelog/branch-aware
  version workflow; `-Preview` selects prerelease behavior. Do not hand-bump a
  stable version on a development branch or execute versioning without consent.
- `New-NovaModulePackage` builds/tests and packages according to `project.json`
  `Package` settings. Configure/review those settings for the intended format.
- `Invoke-NovaRelease -Local -ModuleDirectoryPath '/path/to/test-owned/modules'`
  combines the version/release and local publish lifecycle; it is not needed
  for normal development and is not permission to release.

Never commit, push, tag, publish remotely, install in a user's module directory
or run release/version mutations merely to finish a coding task. Update
`README.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` when behavior changes, keep only real unreleased
entries until release, and normalize edited text to one trailing newline.
